WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.334
-Welcome to the show.
-Hi.

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:03.901
-Thanks for being here.
-Thank you for having me.

00:00:03.901 --> 00:00:05.734
-Congrats on "Los Espookys."

00:00:05.734 --> 00:00:07.968
Season one and season two,
by the way,

00:00:07.968 --> 00:00:10.567
are both 100%
on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:12.334
[ Cheers and applause ]
Just saying. Not bad.

00:00:12.334 --> 00:00:14.300
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.000
-I have some reviews for season
two of "Los Espookys."

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.667
One says "it's hard to think of
a show

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:21.868
that's more outstanding than
'Los Espookys.'"

00:00:21.868 --> 00:00:23.534
That's from Collider.

00:00:23.534 --> 00:00:27.234
A.V. Club says,
"please see this show."

00:00:27.234 --> 00:00:30.200
Indiewire says "'Los Espookys'
may be a difficult show

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.968
to define, but it's an easy show
to appreciate."

00:00:32.968 --> 00:00:36.033
[ Audience oohs ]
-Ooh!

00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:37.367
That sounds good.

00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:38.701
-That sounds good,
and you're in it.

00:00:38.701 --> 00:00:41.067
That's your show, yeah, it does.

00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:42.767
You directed this season.
-I did.

00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:45.834
I directed the last two episodes
of the season.

00:00:45.834 --> 00:00:48.267
It was my first time directing.

00:00:48.267 --> 00:00:50.767
But, you know, it was a role I
felt comfortable stepping into

00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:53.100
because I write and I help
produce and show-run,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.801
so I know it really well.

00:00:54.801 --> 00:00:57.934
So, the directing part was new,
and the big part of it that,

00:00:57.934 --> 00:01:02.133
you know, goes into the process
of directing certain scenes are

00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:03.968
a little more intricate,
are storyboards.

00:01:03.968 --> 00:01:06.868
And I'm not an artist.

00:01:06.868 --> 00:01:10.267
We had an artist come and do
some storyboards for us.

00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:11.534
-This is a storyboard,

00:01:11.534 --> 00:01:13.000
so this is what the scene would
look like.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.334
-This is what the first artist
made.

00:01:15.334 --> 00:01:17.334
And after this first meeting,
I was like, "You know what?

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:20.100
I think I should do
the storyboards,

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.400
just for workflow purposes.
It's easier."

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.868
You know, there's less
sort of telephone

00:01:23.868 --> 00:01:25.601
happening between us
and the artist.

00:01:25.601 --> 00:01:27.033
-You get to directly -- you go,

00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:28.267
"Yeah, I know what the show
should look like."

00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:29.701
-So I was like, after this
meeting, I was like,

00:01:29.701 --> 00:01:32.367
"Let's get rid of
the storyboard artists.

00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:33.767
I'll do the storyboards."
-Yeah.

00:01:33.767 --> 00:01:36.033
And so I did the storyboards
for the rest of the season.

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:37.868
-Yeah, this is great.
Here's your storyboards.

00:01:37.868 --> 00:01:39.267
-And these are my storyboards.

00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:43.300
[ Laughter ]

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:48.367
Obviously, you know, I know
there's a difference in quality.

00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:50.100
-Barely.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.868
If I switched it really fast,
you don't even really know

00:01:52.868 --> 00:01:54.834
which one's yours.
-They got the job done.

00:01:54.834 --> 00:01:57.133
They got the job done,
which is what was important.

00:01:57.133 --> 00:02:00.767
-Yeah. How do you set up
"Los Espookys"?

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:06.667
-I say it's a show about a group
of friends who have a business

00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:10.267
where they stage different types

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:13.934
of stunts to people who need it.

00:02:13.934 --> 00:02:16.701
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:16.701 --> 00:02:19.767
-It's spooky but it's also --
-Wow.

00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:20.934
-Surreal.

00:02:20.934 --> 00:02:22.634
-You just know how to set it up
perfectly.

00:02:22.634 --> 00:02:25.534
-It's a hard show to describe.
It's hard to describe.

00:02:25.534 --> 00:02:27.400
-It is hard, but it's so funny.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:28.467
It's brilliant.

00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:30.634
And it's Fred Armisen,
Julio Torres.

00:02:30.634 --> 00:02:33.267
Is this the first time
you worked with Fred?

00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:35.000
-On a show like this, yes.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.400
I had met Fred in 2016.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.634
"Portlandia" was looking for
a writer, and I was asked

00:02:41.634 --> 00:02:44.400
if I wanted to submit,
and I didn't have

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.234
any scripts
or anything like that.

00:02:46.234 --> 00:02:49.868
I had never written a script,
but I had a Zine,

00:02:49.868 --> 00:02:52.767
and so I submitted my Zine.

00:02:52.767 --> 00:02:55.300
And I think when the producer
was going through, you know,

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.901
all these scripts and saw
the cover of my Zine,

00:02:57.901 --> 00:03:00.968
was like, "What is this?"
and then started reading it,

00:03:00.968 --> 00:03:03.300
and they liked it and shared it
with Fred and Carrie.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.267
It's called
"The Truth About Pangaea."

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:07.934
-This is your Zine.
-Yeah.

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:09.501
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:09.501 --> 00:03:11.601
This got you hired.

00:03:11.601 --> 00:03:13.334
-It did not get me hired.
-There you go.

00:03:13.334 --> 00:03:15.767
-However, it got the --
that's how I met Fred.

00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:17.367
Fred was like,
"This is really funny."

00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:18.534
And then we stayed in touch

00:03:18.534 --> 00:03:20.701
and made little videos
and things together.

00:03:20.701 --> 00:03:24.634
And when he got the idea to --
for what became "Los Espookys,"

00:03:24.634 --> 00:03:27.234
he asked Julio and I if we
wanted to help to develop it.

00:03:27.234 --> 00:03:29.267
-This wasn't the first show

00:03:29.267 --> 00:03:31.167
that you've submitted for it
and didn't get the job.

00:03:31.167 --> 00:03:33.000
-Correct.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.567
-Another show was
"The Tonight Show."

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:37.167
-Yeah.
[ Audience oohs ]

00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:39.868
No, it's okay. It's okay.

00:03:39.868 --> 00:03:43.667
In January of 2015, I submitted
to be a writer here.

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:45.601
And I did not get the job.

00:03:45.601 --> 00:03:48.834
But because I'm here, and you
don't often get the opportunity

00:03:48.834 --> 00:03:50.234
to find out
when you don't get a job

00:03:50.234 --> 00:03:53.267
why you didn't get the job,
I just wanted to ask sort of

00:03:53.267 --> 00:03:55.067
what it was about
some of the material.

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:56.734
[ Laughter ]
[ Drumroll ]

00:03:56.734 --> 00:03:58.434
-Stop, stop, stop.

00:03:58.434 --> 00:04:00.834
-I brought part of the things
that I submitted.

00:04:00.834 --> 00:04:02.868
[ Laughter ]
[ Audience ohs ]

00:04:02.868 --> 00:04:04.534
-Sometimes it doesn't get to me,
you know?

00:04:04.534 --> 00:04:07.100
Sometimes it goes
to other people.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:10.234
-Well, I just want to use the
opportunity to see sort of what

00:04:10.234 --> 00:04:12.400
I did wrong
and how I could do better.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.434
So, these were some of the desk
bits that were in my packet.

00:04:15.434 --> 00:04:18.367
-Okay.
-One is called "Non-Slip."

00:04:18.367 --> 00:04:21.167
"In this bit, an audience member
tests how effective

00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:23.367
a pair of shoes marketed as
non-slip are.

00:04:23.367 --> 00:04:25.300
They must power walk
through an obstacle course

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.000
on an oily and/or wet floor.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.667
If they slip and fall, we know
the shoes are not effective.

00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:31.901
A video recording of
the quality control test

00:04:31.901 --> 00:04:33.734
may be sent to
the shoe's manufacturer."

00:04:33.734 --> 00:04:36.734
[ Laughter ]

00:04:36.734 --> 00:04:39.801
-I can't believe -- I can't
believe we passed on this.

00:04:39.801 --> 00:04:41.934
-Okay, yeah. And then you passed
on this too.

00:04:41.934 --> 00:04:44.634
It's called "You're Hired."
"In this bit, viewers list Jimmy

00:04:44.634 --> 00:04:46.868
as a reference on
a job application.

00:04:46.868 --> 00:04:49.334
If Jimmy gets a call from
a company inquiring about

00:04:49.334 --> 00:04:51.567
a job applicant, he must try
and make the applicant seem

00:04:51.567 --> 00:04:52.968
like a good fit for the job.

00:04:52.968 --> 00:04:55.501
The calls are recorded
and played on the show."

00:04:55.501 --> 00:04:57.534
[ Laughter ]

00:04:57.534 --> 00:05:00.868
-So, I just have a camera on me
at all times just in case

00:05:00.868 --> 00:05:02.367
someone calls me
for a reference?

00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:03.667
-Correct.
-Okay.

00:05:03.667 --> 00:05:05.434
-Okay. And then there was one
called "Mike Check."

00:05:05.434 --> 00:05:07.033
-"Mike Check."
What's "Mike Check"?

00:05:07.033 --> 00:05:09.067
-"In this bit,
Jimmy says 'mike check?'

00:05:09.067 --> 00:05:11.000
Everyone in the audience
named Mike has to stand up

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.100
for the rest of the show."

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:17.901
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:17.901 --> 00:05:20.334
-Actually, do you want to do
that tonight?

00:05:20.334 --> 00:05:23.267
-Okay.
-Wait. Wait, this is fantastic.

00:05:23.267 --> 00:05:24.567
Can we do this tonight?

00:05:24.567 --> 00:05:25.801
I don't know if we can give you
writing credits.

00:05:25.801 --> 00:05:27.167
Do you mind?
-No, it's okay.

00:05:27.167 --> 00:05:31.634
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Can I get this mike working?

00:05:31.634 --> 00:05:33.267
One, two, three.

00:05:33.267 --> 00:05:35.734
-Mike check!

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:37.133
[ Rim shot ]

00:05:37.133 --> 00:05:39.534
[ Laughter and applause ]
-One guy.

00:05:39.534 --> 00:05:42.467
There it is. Mike!

00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:44.534
One guy named Mike.

00:05:44.534 --> 00:05:50.100
[ Applause ]

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.100
-For the rest of the show --
-For the rest of the show,

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.567
you have to stay standing, Mike.

00:05:53.567 --> 00:05:55.901
That's the bit, sorry.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:55.901 --> 00:05:57.334
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:05:57.334 --> 00:06:00.634
Here's Ana Fabrega in the new
season of "Los Espookys."

00:06:00.634 --> 00:06:02.133
Take a look at this.

00:06:02.133 --> 00:06:03.667
[ Conversing in Spanish ]

00:06:30.968 --> 00:06:36.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:36.067 --> 00:06:38.300
-Mike!
-Mike! Mike!

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:39.534
-There he is.

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:43.000
Ana Fabrega, everyone.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:47.934
New episodes of "Los Espookys"
airs Friday on HBO and HBO Max.

00:06:47.934 --> 00:06:50.033
We'll be right back with
stand-up from Michael Rowland.

00:06:50.033 --> 00:06:51.868
Stick around, everybody.

00:06:51.868 --> 00:07:01.801
♪♪

